          45S 80E, Southern Indian Ocean, rendered from that day’s atmospheric data.
          <a href="https://art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-08-06-060044/region-45s-80e/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="underline hover:opacity-70">View the painting</a>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <h2 class="e-h2">The Drawer, Not the Bird</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">The best vocabulary for this problem was coined by Darwin, in a letter to Hooker in 1857, and he was complaining about colleagues. Those who make many species, he wrote, are the “splitters,” and those who make few are the “lumpers.” He meant it lightly. It has outlasted almost everything else in the letter, because the distinction it names turns out to be permanent.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Here is how a species is anchored. Somewhere there is a type specimen: a single dead individual in a drawer, usually in London or Washington or Leiden. The name is attached not to the population in the field but to that one carcass. Everything else is argument. Whether the birds on this island and the birds on that island are one species or two is settled by committees, which meet, deliberate and vote, and have been known to reverse themselves twice within a working lifetime. The world’s bird species number about eleven thousand, except that until the unified AviList of 2025 the two main checklists disagreed with each other by several hundred. A well-known 2016 analysis argued that under a stricter definition of species the true figure is closer to eighteen thousand. That is not a rounding error. It is seven thousand kinds of animal appearing and vanishing according to which definition is in fashion, while not one bird in the field moves an inch or notices anything at all.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">That could be the whole essay, but it is not, because of one difference the bird cannot supply. The bird does not read the checklist. It has never been told of its promotion and has no view on the committee. It will breed or fail to breed on exactly the terms it would have used had the volume never been printed. The philosopher Ian Hacking called these <em>indifferent kinds</em>. Quarks and mosses and weather systems do not consult their classification, which is exactly why the classification can be treated as a description of them.</p>
        <p>The other sort he called <em>interactive kinds</em>, and the entire difficulty lies in the difference. When you classify a person, the person may find out. Having found out, they may agree, resist, organize, apply for something, change their account of their own history, or simply begin to notice things about themselves that they had never put under a heading. The classified thing changes, which changes what is true of the class, which changes the classification, which can be read again. Hacking called this the <em>looping effect</em>, and he spent a career on the observation that in the human sciences the instrument is never merely an instrument. A drawer that talks back is not a drawer.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">The Enumerated Self</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">This brings us back to the last page of the application, and to the fact that it does not ask an abstract question.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">The current disability form does not say “Do you have a disability?” and leave you to it. It prints a list that includes the following. Alcohol or other substance use disorder. Autoimmune disorder, including lupus, fibromyalgia and rheumatoid arthritis. Blind or low vision. Cancer, past or present. Celiac disease. Deaf or hard of hearing. Depression or anxiety. Diabetes. Epilepsy. Gastrointestinal disorders. Mental health conditions, including bipolar disorder, schizophrenia, post-traumatic stress disorder and obsessive compulsive disorder. Missing limbs. Nervous system conditions, including migraine headaches. Neurodivergence, including attention deficit hyperactivity disorder, autism spectrum disorder and dyslexia. Short stature. Traumatic brain injury.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Read that as an applicant rather than as a compliance officer. It is not the language of measurement. Measurement asks a question and waits. This list enumerates and invites recognition, which is how a screening questionnaire works, and how a clinician works at intake. The difference is that a clinician has fifteen minutes, a medical history and a duty of care. This list is administered by a payroll vendor to a person who has just typed their own name for the fourth time and would like to know about the health plan.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">So does it do anything? Has anyone ever read that page and gone looking for a diagnosis? The honest answer is that the mechanism is well understood and the direction of travel is not in serious doubt. Migraine, depression and adult attention deficit are conditions with real biology, wide prevalence and a lot of diagnostic latitude at the margins. A person’s decision to seek assessment is strongly influenced by whether the category has been offered to them in a form that seems to fit. Hacking’s original case study was multiple personality, where the description’s availability and its circulation through clinics and paperback memoirs came before, and produced, the statistics. You do not have to accept that reading in full to accept the general point, which is modest: categories that are printed, official, repeated annually and attached to a benefit will be lived in more than categories that are not.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">The same is true, more quietly, of the demographic page. The cleanest example in American statistics is the word “Hispanic,” which was manufactured. Before the mid-1970s the federal government had no such category, and Mexican Americans, Puerto Ricans and Cuban Americans did not generally think of themselves as one kind of people. A congressional act in 1976 and a directive from the Office of Management and Budget (OMB) in 1977 created the classification for statistical purposes. Activists, broadcasters and marketers then had an interest in filling it. Within two decades a real shared identity existed where there had been none, with its own politics and its own media market. Cristina Mora’s history of the process is called <em>Making Hispanics</em>, and the title is not a provocation. It is a description.</p>
        <p>The process has not stopped. In March 2024 the OMB revised the standards again, made a single combined race and ethnicity question the default, and added a Middle Eastern or North African category. Several million people became a new kind of American by administrative revision, having done nothing whatever, like a bird promoted to full species while asleep. The difference, once again, is that they can read the notice.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="my-14">
